::STAT_U03_L01_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which statement best describes correlation?
{
=A. A relationship between two numerical variables#Correlation describes how two numerical variables are related.
~B. Proof that one variable causes another#Correlation does not prove causation.
~C. A table with one categorical variable#That describes univariate categorical data, not correlation.
~D. A random assignment method#Random assignment is a study design feature, not correlation.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A scatterplot shows that higher outdoor temperature is linked with more water bottles sold. What conclusion is safest?
{
~A. Temperature proves every person buys water#This is too absolute and claims more than the data show.
~B. Water bottle sales cause hot weather#This reverses the likely relationship and is not supported.
=C. Temperature is associated with water bottle sales#This accurately describes the relationship shown.
~D. There is no association#The described pattern shows an association.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
As hours of video gaming increase, homework completion tends to decrease. What direction is the association?
{
=A. Negative#One variable increases while the other tends to decrease.
~B. Positive#Positive means both variables tend to increase together.
~C. No clear association#The statement describes a clear pattern.
~D. Causal by definition#Direction of association does not prove causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which question helps identify a possible confounding variable?
{
~A. Are the variables spelled correctly?#Spelling does not evaluate the statistical relationship.
~B. Is the graph colorful?#Color does not determine whether a relationship is causal.
~C. Did the data use decimals?#Decimals do not identify another explanatory variable.
=D. Could another factor explain both variables?#A confounding variable may explain the association.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A survey shows students with part-time jobs report better time management. What should you avoid claiming?
{
~A. The variables may be associated#This is a careful claim that may be supported.
~B. There may be another explanation#This is a responsible caution.
=C. Part-time jobs caused better time management#A survey association does not prove the job caused the skill.
~D. The result is about the surveyed students#This appropriately limits the conclusion.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which phrase is usually safest when a study only shows a pattern?
{
~A. is guaranteed by#Guarantee is too strong for an observed pattern.
~B. is always caused by#Always caused by is an unsupported causal claim.
=C. is associated with#This describes a pattern without overclaiming causation.
~D. has no connection to#A pattern suggests some connection may exist.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Two categorical variables are compared in a table. Can association be discussed?
{
~A. No, association only applies to equations#Association can be discussed for categorical data too.
=B. Yes, categorical data can show association#Categorical variables can be associated in a two-way table.
~C. No, tables cannot show relationships#Two-way tables can show relationships between categories.
~D. Yes, and it always proves causation#Association in categorical data still does not prove causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A researcher wants to support a causal claim. Which design feature is most helpful?
{
~A. A bigger font on the graph#Visual style does not support causation.
~B. A single survey question#A single survey question is usually weak for causation.
=C. Random assignment in an experiment#Random assignment helps separate treatment effects from other factors.
~D. Ignoring possible confounders#Ignoring confounders makes causal claims weaker.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which statement is an example of a causal claim?
{
~A. Extra tutoring is related to scores#This is association language.
~B. Extra tutoring and scores were recorded#This only describes data collection.
~C. Extra tutoring varied among students#This describes variation, not causation.
=D. Extra tutoring caused the score increase#The word caused makes this a causal claim.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A pattern slopes upward from left to right. What association is shown?
{
=A. Positive association#Upward patterns show positive association.
~B. Negative association#Negative association slopes downward.
~C. No association#A clear upward pattern shows association.
~D. Impossible association#An upward pattern is a common association.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Why is "correlation proves causation" incorrect?
{
~A. Correlation never uses two variables#Correlation does use two numerical variables.
~B. Causation is always impossible#Causation can be supported by stronger study designs.
=C. Another variable may explain the relationship#Confounding variables can create or affect an observed correlation.
~D. Correlation only applies to colors#Correlation applies to numerical variables, not colors specifically.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which conclusion is most careful?
{
~A. The app caused every student to improve#This is too strong and causal.
=B. App use is associated with higher grades in the data#This is careful and contextual.
~C. The app guarantees higher grades#Guarantee is too strong for statistical evidence.
~D. The app has no relationship to grades#This ignores a possible pattern if one was observed.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
What must be identified first when describing association?
{
=A. The two variables being compared#Association describes a relationship between variables.
~B. The color of the chart#Chart color does not define the relationship.
~C. The name of the calculator#Technology is not needed to identify variables.
~D. The final grade only#One variable alone is not enough for association.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A data set shows no visible pattern between shoe size and quiz score. What is the best conclusion?
{
~A. Shoe size causes quiz score#No pattern and no causal evidence support this.
=B. No clear association is shown#The data do not show a visible relationship.
~C. Quiz score causes shoe size#This is unsupported and unreasonable.
~D. The association is strongly positive#A strong positive association would show an upward pattern.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which phrase signals an unsupported overclaim when only association is shown?
{
~A. tends to be related to#This is careful association language.
~B. appears connected with#This is careful because it avoids proving causation.
~C. is associated with#This is appropriate for observed relationships.
=D. definitely causes#Definitely causes is too strong unless causal evidence exists.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A graph shows study time and test score rising together. What can be said?
{
~A. Study time has no connection to score#The rising pattern suggests a relationship.
=B. Study time and score have a positive association#Both variables tend to increase together.
~C. Score definitely causes study time#This reverses causation and is unsupported.
~D. Study time guarantees a perfect score#A trend does not guarantee an individual result.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which situation is most likely bivariate?
{
~A. Listing only favorite colors#That is one categorical variable.
~B. Counting only the number of students present#That is one count, not paired variables.
~C. Naming the school mascot#That is not a bivariate data set.
=D. Recording each student's height and backpack weight#Two variables are measured for each student.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A health article says sleeping more is linked with lower stress. What should a careful reader ask?
{
~A. Is the title written in bold?#Bold text does not evaluate the claim.
=B. Does the evidence support causation or only association?#This checks whether the conclusion matches the study evidence.
~C. Are the numbers written in alphabetical order?#Alphabetical order is not relevant to causation.
~D. Did every person have the same name?#Names do not evaluate the statistical relationship.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which conclusion goes beyond the evidence if data only show an association?
{
~A. The variables may be related#This stays within association language.
~B. The pattern is positive#This describes direction if supported by the data.
~C. The relationship should be interpreted in context#This is good statistical practice.
=D. One variable caused the other#Causation requires stronger evidence than association alone.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
What is a confounding variable?
{
=A. Another factor that may explain an observed relationship#Confounders can affect how association is interpreted.
~B. The answer choice marked correct#That is test structure, not statistics.
~C. A variable that is always ignored safely#Ignoring it can lead to wrong conclusions.
~D. A variable that proves there is no association#A confounder does not automatically remove association.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which word best replaces "causes" when evidence is only observational?
{
~A. forces#This is causal and too strong.
=B. is associated with#Observational evidence usually supports association, not automatic causation.
~C. guarantees#This is too absolute for statistical evidence.
~D. eliminates#This changes the meaning and is unsupported.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
An experiment randomly assigns students to two study methods. Why does this help causal reasoning?
{
~A. It makes the graph colorful#Graph appearance does not support causation.
~B. It removes the need for data#Experiments still require data.
~C. It guarantees every student improves#Random assignment does not guarantee individual outcomes.
=D. It helps balance other factors between groups#Random assignment reduces the effect of confounding variables.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which statement correctly describes negative association?
{
=A. One variable tends to decrease as the other increases#This describes a negative relationship.
~B. Both variables increase together#That describes positive association.
~C. There is no pattern at all#No pattern means no clear association.
~D. One variable must cause the other#Association direction does not prove causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Why should conclusions include context?
{
=A. Context explains what the variables mean in the real situation#Statistical conclusions should be tied to the situation studied.
~B. Context makes all causal claims true#Context helps interpretation but does not prove causation.
~C. Context replaces the need for variables#Variables are still needed.
~D. Context makes every association positive#Direction depends on the data, not context alone.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which is the best rewrite of "Music causes better concentration" if only survey data were collected?
{
~A. Music forces all students to concentrate#This is too strong and causal.
~B. Concentration proves music was playing#This reverses the relationship and overclaims.
=C. Music listening is associated with reported concentration#This keeps the conclusion within what survey association can support.
~D. Music has no possible relationship to concentration#A survey pattern may support association.
}
